Done. The off-by-one is fixed and verified.

## Root cause

```c
for (int i = 0; i < n / 2; i++) {
    char t = s[i];
    s[i]     = s[n - i];      /* BUG: index n, not n-1 */
    s[n - i] = t;
}
```

`s[n - i]` is off by one. For `"abc"` (`n = 3`, `i = 0`) the swap targets `s[3]` — which is the NUL terminator (and one byte past the array's last character). First iteration swapped `'a'` ↔ `'\0'`,
